once, at the end — they are *bytes*, not chars), plus `\"`, `\\`, `\t`, `\n`, `\r`. **Everything else refuses — returns `None`, so the file is dropped exactly as it was before, never guessed at:** a byte sequence that is not valid UTF-8, an escape git is not known to emit, an octal run shorter than three digits, a lone trailing backslash, an unterminated quoted token, and a header whose second token carries trailing junk. Inventing a path that is not in the diff is the failure mode round 81's refusal branch already chose against, and this branch makes the same choice for the same reason. **#830 remains open and unfixed, and that is the deliberate half:** a path containing a literal `" b/"` still makes an *unquoted* header genuinely ambiguous, and `diff_header_path` still refuses rather than guessing — closing it needs a design decision about which anchor to trust, so it was filed rather than smuggled in here, and its refusal row is pinned by test so this task could not weaken it while adding the quoted branch. Fixtures were captured verbatim from real `git show` output in a scratch repo rather than hand-typed (round 81 lost a bet to exactly that shortcut): a 100%-similarity rename `diff --git "a/n\303\244me.txt" "b/n\303\240me2.txt"` → `feat(nàme2): update code`, an escaped quote in the filename, a modified and a deleted quoted path (both via the hunk lines), a quoted binary change, and git's trailing-tab separator on `+++` lines. Near-miss guards pin that the spaced unquoted path, an ordinary modification, an added file and a whole-file deletion are all byte-identical to before — a discriminator tested only where it fires is vacuous green. Positive control run rather than assumed: neutering the unquoting branch fails exactly the new quoted tests and leaves every near-miss guard green.
